"""Week45 Days 1-2: interpretable numerical prediction and honest metrics."""

L(0, '1. Predict a quantity and define its unit',
  'Frame a regression target and distinguish an estimate from an exact rule.',
  [('Regression target; predicted value', 'Regression predicts a numerical quantity. A model estimate can be fractional even when the observed quantity is a count.'),
   ('Target unit; residual', 'The unit tells you what a number means. In this week residual = actual minus predicted; a positive residual means the model underpredicted.')],
  'Week 44 introduced supervised learning and safe evaluation boundaries. This week we predict amounts rather than category codes. A demand estimate of 112.6 cups is a numerical prediction; it is not the category called112 and it is not a promise that exactly112.6 cups will be consumed.\n\nStart with the prediction time. For an event, registered guests, planned duration, a temperature forecast and a planned promotion can exist beforehand. Later actual cups served belong to the target. Using the final observed attendance or actual sales at prediction time would change the question and can leak future information.\n\nOur project uses independent fictional events generated locally. The simulator gives us a controllable learning exercise, not evidence about a real café. If an exact policy or demand formula were known in a real application, use that rule rather than approximate it with ML. We deliberately hide no real customer data inside this lesson. Keep units and this educational scope visible throughout your report.',
  'actual = [100, 120, 90]\npredicted = [96.5, 126.0, 87.5]\nfor a, p in zip(actual, predicted):\n    print("actual", a, "predicted", p, "residual", a - p, "cups")',
  'For actual80 and predicted72.5, calculate the residual and explain the direction.',
  'actual, predicted = 80, 72.5\nresidual = actual - predicted\nprint("Residual:", residual, "cups")\nprint("Underprediction:", residual > 0)',
  'Separate known-before-event fields from an observed-after-event field.',
  'known = {"registered_guests": True, "planned_hours": True, "forecast_c": True, "actual_cups": False}\nprint("Allowed inputs:", [name for name, available in known.items() if available])',
  [('What does a positive actual-minus-predicted residual mean?', 'The prediction was too low', 'The prediction was too high', 'The model was exactly right', 'Actual exceeded the prediction.'),
   ('Must regression output always be an integer when the target is a count?', 'No; it can be a numerical estimate', 'Always', 'Only after changing the target labels', 'An estimate of expected demand can be fractional; planning and rounding are separate decisions.')],
  'Call a predicted amount exact future demand and use actual future sales as an input.', 'State the unit, uncertainty and before-event feature availability.',
  'Each printed residual keeps its sign and cup unit. The direction tells you whether supply planned from that estimate might be too low or too high; a score later summarises these individual errors.')

L(0, '2. Fit a line and read slope and intercept',
  'Use LinearRegression and explain every term of a one-feature prediction.',
  [('LinearRegression; ordinary least squares', 'This estimator fits coefficients and an intercept to minimise training squared residuals. It is a regressor, not the classification estimator used last week.'),
   ('coef_; intercept_; slope', 'coef_ stores fitted slopes and intercept_ the fitted constant. With one feature, predicted y = intercept + slope times x.')],
  'A straight-line model is a useful first numerical predictor because its calculation is visible. In this exact teaching table, every additional planned hour adds four cups and the constant is ten cups. LinearRegression recovers that relationship from the supplied pairs. Real measurements usually contain noise, so the line will not pass through every row.\n\nX remains two-dimensional: each row contains one hour measurement. y contains one demand answer per row. fit learns the line; predict applies it to a new row. The line chart places observed points and the fitted prediction in the same units so you can check the arithmetic visually.\n\nAn intercept is the model prediction when its inputs are zero. That zero may not be an observed or meaningful situation. Here it is part of a deliberately exact teaching relationship; it does not establish a real venue’s fixed demand. Do not interpret every fitted constant as a physical law, or use the plot as evidence of causation.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nX = np.array([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0], [4.0]])\ny = np.array([14.0, 18.0, 22.0, 26.0])\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint("Slope:", round(float(m.coef_[0]), 3))\nprint("Intercept:", round(float(m.intercept_), 3))\nprint("At2.5hours:", round(float(m.predict([[2.5]])[0]), 3))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.7))\nax.scatter(X.ravel(), y, label="Observed teaching pairs", color="#d27a32")\nax.plot(X.ravel(), m.predict(X), label="Fitted line", color="#376ea6")\nax.set(xlabel="Planned hours", ylabel="Cups", title="Read a fitted slope and intercept")\nax.legend()\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Fit the exact relationship y=5+3x from three pairs and predict at x=4.',
  'from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], [8.0, 11.0, 14.0])\nprint("Slope:", round(float(m.coef_[0]), 3))\nprint("Intercept:", round(float(m.intercept_), 3))\nprint("At4:", round(float(m.predict([[4.0]])[0]), 3))',
  'Reproduce the estimator’s prediction using its fitted arithmetic.',
  'from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], [8.0, 11.0, 14.0])\nmanual = m.intercept_ + m.coef_[0] * 2.5\nprint("Manual:", round(float(manual), 3))\nprint("Estimator:", round(float(m.predict([[2.5]])[0]), 3))',
  [('What does the slope unit mean here?', 'Cups per additional hour in this fitted model', 'Hours per cup automatically', 'Probability of a class', 'The target unit divided by the feature unit gives the slope unit.'),
   ('Does fit produce perfect agreement for every noisy dataset?', 'No; it minimises squared training residuals', 'Always', 'Only with four rows', 'A least-squares line need not pass through all observations.')],
  'Read coef_ as a probability and treat every intercept as an observed real-world fact.', 'Read slope and intercept in their model and unit context.',
  'The four observations are exactly10+4x, so the fitted values match them. At2.5, the arithmetic is10+4×2.5=20cups. This exact agreement belongs to the constructed example.',
  syntax='prediction = fitted_intercept + fitted_slope * feature_value',
  figure=('Four exact teaching observations and their fitted regression line', 'Both axes retain original units. The exact line belongs to this constructed relationship; real demand usually has residual error.'))

L(0, '3. Understand least squares by counting residual costs',
  'Compute squared residual cost and explain why sign cancellation is misleading.',
  [('Squared-error objective; SSE', 'The sum of squared errors adds residual squared across training rows. Squaring prevents opposite signs cancelling and emphasises large misses.'),
   ('Objective versus reporting metric', 'The rule an estimator minimises during fitting can differ from the metric used to compare models. That distinction must be stated.')],
  'A raw average signed residual can be zero even when every prediction is wrong. If one prediction is ten cups too high and another ten too low, their signs cancel. Least squares instead squares each residual before summing, so both contribute positive cost.\n\nLinearRegression chooses its coefficients to minimise this squared training cost among the lines it can represent. That does not mean its training error is an honest estimate of future error, nor that squared cost matches every practical planning goal. You will report held-out MAE, MSE, RMSE and R-squared in Day2.\n\nThe example compares three deliberately chosen prediction vectors against the same actual values. This isolates the cost definition without implying that hand-picking predictions is a training algorithm. Explain why one very large miss can matter more under squared loss than several small ones. Keep the evaluated rows identical when comparing costs.',
  'import numpy as np\ny = np.array([10.0, 20.0, 30.0])\nguesses = {"opposite errors": [5.0, 20.0, 35.0], "one big error": [10.0, 20.0, 40.0], "small errors": [9.0, 19.0, 29.0]}\nfor name, values in guesses.items():\n    residual = y - np.array(values)\n    print(name, "mean signed", round(float(residual.mean()), 3), "SSE", float((residual ** 2).sum()))',
  'Show that residuals[-10,10] average to zero but have squared cost200.',
  'import numpy as np\nr = np.array([-10.0, 10.0])\nprint("Signed mean:", float(r.mean()))\nprint("SSE:", float((r ** 2).sum()))',
  'Compare equal absolute-error totals with different squared costs.',
  'import numpy as np\nfor errors in [[5.0, 5.0], [0.0, 10.0]]:\n    e = np.array(errors)\n    print(errors, "absolute total", float(np.abs(e).sum()), "squared total", float((e ** 2).sum()))',
  [('Why not use only the signed average residual as an error magnitude?', 'Opposite mistakes can cancel', 'Negative residuals are invalid', 'It is always larger than RMSE', 'Zero signed average does not imply accurate individual predictions.'),
   ('What does ordinary least squares minimise during fitting?', 'Squared residual sum on training rows', 'Final test error without seeing the test', 'Every possible deployment cost', 'Its optimisation objective is defined on the data supplied to fit.')],
  'A zero signed average means every prediction is good.', 'Inspect absolute or squared errors as well as signed bias.',
  'The two opposite five-cup misses cancel in signed mean but contribute25+25 to SSE. The one ten-cup miss contributes100. Squared loss weights error size nonlinearly.')

L(0, '4. Interpret multiple coefficients without a causal story',
  'Explain a multi-feature linear equation and its conditional coefficients.',
  [('Multiple linear regression', 'A prediction combines an intercept with one coefficient per feature. A coefficient describes the fitted change for that feature while other supplied feature values are held fixed.'),
   ('Collinearity; identifiability', 'Strongly related columns can make individual coefficients unstable or non-unique, even when predictions are similar. Coefficient magnitude alone is not causal importance.')],
  'With several inputs, predicted demand is b0+b1×guests+b2×hours, and so on. A guest coefficient has cups-per-guest units; an hour coefficient has cups-per-hour units. Comparing their raw magnitudes as importance ignores that the features use different units and distributions.\n\nThe teaching pairs below are built from a known exact relationship with two independently varied inputs. Holding hours fixed, increasing guests by one changes the fitted prediction by two cups. This is an interpretation of the fitted equation. In real observational data, it does not prove that an intervention on guest count or promotion causes that change.\n\nIf you include two versions of the same information, such as guests and twice-guests, the individual coefficients are not uniquely determined by prediction fit alone. Numerical solvers may return one solution, but interpreting each as a separate physical effect would be unsound. Regularisation in Day3 helps control coefficient instability; it does not establish causation.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX = np.array([[10, 1], [10, 2], [20, 1], [20, 2]], dtype=float)\ny = 5 + 2 * X[:, 0] + 3 * X[:, 1]\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint("guests/hours coefficients:", m.coef_.round(3).tolist())\nprint("Intercept:", round(float(m.intercept_), 3))\nprint("For15guests and2hours:", round(float(m.predict([[15, 2]])[0]), 3))',
  'Compare predictions when guests increase from15 to16 at the same two-hour duration.',
  'from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit([[10, 1], [10, 2], [20, 1], [20, 2]], [28, 31, 48, 51])\np = m.predict([[15, 2], [16, 2]])\nprint("Model change:", round(float(p[1] - p[0]), 3), "cups")',
  'Show that guests and twice-guests are redundant columns.',
  'import numpy as np\nX = np.array([[10, 20], [20, 40], [30, 60]], dtype=float)\nprint("Second is twice first:", bool(np.allclose(X[:, 1], 2 * X[:, 0])))\nprint("Do not claim two independent effects")',
  [('How is a fitted coefficient interpreted with other features fixed?', 'As a change in the model prediction per feature unit', 'As guaranteed causal truth', 'As a class probability', 'The statement describes the fitted equation under its chosen inputs.'),
   ('Are raw coefficients directly comparable across guests and hours?', 'No; units and representation differ', 'Always', 'Only if both are positive', 'Scale and correlation affect coefficient interpretation.')],
  'Rank raw coefficients as proven causal importance regardless of units or related columns.', 'Interpret conditional prediction changes, feature units and dependence explicitly.',
  'The known teaching relation is5+2×guests+3×hours. Independent variation identifies these coefficients here. The arithmetic at15guests and2hours gives41cups.')

L(0, '5. Check residual structure and state assumptions',
  'Use a residual plot to notice a poorly matched linear representation.',
  [('Residual diagnostic', 'A plot of actual-minus-predicted values against predictions or features. Patterns suggest a missing relationship, changed noise scale or data issue; they do not prove a cause.'),
   ('Linearity; independent errors; equal error variance', 'These are useful modelling/diagnostic considerations. Normal errors are not required merely to run least squares; stronger assumptions are needed for some classical uncertainty claims.')],
  'A line can fit poorly when the relationship curves. If low and high feature values have positive residuals while the middle has negative residuals, a curved feature may help. The plot below uses an exact quadratic teaching relationship, so the straight-line miss is visible rather than hidden inside a headline score.\n\nInspecting training residuals is a development diagnostic, not final evaluation. A small training error does not certify generalisation. Related observations, changing error spread, outliers and measurement mistakes need investigation. A residual plot can suggest a hypothesis but cannot establish why the pattern occurred.\n\nLinearRegression does not automatically produce valid prediction intervals or p-values. Ordinary prediction fitting and classical inferential assumptions are different subjects. We use diagnostics to improve a reviewed representation and keep model comparison inside development data. Avoid claiming normality is a requirement for calling fit or claiming a plot has verified every statistical assumption.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nX = np.linspace(-2, 2, 21).reshape(-1, 1)\ny = 10 + 3 * X[:, 0] ** 2\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nresidual = y - m.predict(X)\nprint("Residual near edge/centre:", residual[[0, 10, 20]].round(3).tolist())\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.7))\nax.scatter(X[:, 0], residual, color="#376ea6")\nax.axhline(0, color="#555555", linewidth=1)\nax.set(xlabel="Teaching feature", ylabel="Actual minus predicted", title="Curvature left behind by a straight line")\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Fit a straight line to [0,1,2] with target[1,2,5] and print signed residuals.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nX = np.array([[0.0], [1.0], [2.0]])\ny = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 5.0])\nm = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)\nprint("Residuals:", (y - m.predict(X)).round(3).tolist())',
  'Write three checks that a successful fit call cannot prove.',
  'for concern in ["independence of rows", "correct measurement units", "valid future prediction intervals"]:\n    print("Still investigate:", concern)',
  [('Does calling LinearRegression.fit require normally distributed target values?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for four features', 'Running least squares does not require a Gaussian target; inferential claims have separate conditions.'),
   ('What does a residual curve suggest?', 'A relationship may be missing from the representation', 'The cause is proven', 'The final test has passed', 'Diagnostics suggest development hypotheses rather than certify all assumptions.')],
  'A fit call succeeded, so all model assumptions and uncertainty statements are valid.', 'Inspect appropriate diagnostics and qualify what the fitted model can support.',
  'The target varies quadratically while the fitted line cannot represent that curvature. The symmetric residual pattern records that mismatch on training examples; Day3 adds curved representations.',
  figure=('U-shaped training residuals from fitting a line to a quadratic relationship', 'The pattern belongs to this constructed training example. It motivates a curved representation, not a causal conclusion or a final holdout score.'))

L(0, '6. Recognise extrapolation and prediction limits',
  'Explain why an interpretable line can still fail outside observed input support.',
  [('Interpolation; extrapolation', 'Interpolation uses a model within the observed input region. Extrapolation applies it beyond that region, where the fitted relationship may no longer hold.'),
   ('Unconstrained output', 'Ordinary linear regression can predict negative values or values beyond plausible demand. Input validation and domain-aware modelling are separate from fitting a line.')],
  'A neat equation does not know a venue’s capacity or a person’s physiology. Extending a fitted trend far outside the observed range can produce absurd results. Even inside a training range, prediction can fail when other features or the future population differ.\n\nThe example learns a decreasing teaching line from one to three hours. At a much larger input it predicts a negative quantity. That is valid arithmetic for the fitted equation but an invalid physical demand amount. Clipping it to zero changes the prediction procedure and does not create evidence that the underlying model is useful.\n\nRecord training support and intended use, make units visible, and review out-of-range requests. Do not silently mix a clipping or rounding rule into the final metric after choosing it from test errors. If a postprocessing rule is needed, declare it before evaluation and evaluate that complete procedure. Our final lab reports raw, unrounded numerical estimates and explicitly limits them to an educational simulation.',
  'from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nm = LinearRegression().fit([[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], [18.0, 16.0, 14.0])\nprint("Within range:", round(float(m.predict([[2.5]])[0]), 3))\nprint("Far outside range:", round(float(m.predict([[20.0]])[0]), 3))\nprint("Negative demand is not physically meaningful")',
  'Flag a requested duration outside a known one-to-five-hour training region.',
  'observed_min, observed_max = 1.0, 5.0\nfor hours in [3.0, 20.0]:\n    print(hours, "outside support:", not observed_min <= hours <= observed_max)',
  'Show that rounding changes an estimate and therefore belongs in the declared prediction procedure.',
  'prediction = 112.6\nprint("Raw estimate:", prediction)\nprint("Rounded planning value:", round(prediction))\nprint("Score the stated procedure consistently")',
  [('Does a positive slope prove it remains valid arbitrarily far beyond training?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with a high training R-squared', 'A fitted trend has no automatic evidence outside its observed setting.'),
   ('Does clipping a negative demand prediction validate the model?', 'No; it only changes the output rule', 'Yes', 'Only when rounded too', 'Postprocessing cannot supply missing generalisation evidence.')],
  'Extend a line indefinitely and treat every numeric output as plausible physical demand.', 'Review support, units and domain constraints and evaluate any declared postprocessing.',
  'The fitted line is20−2×hours. Its negative far-range result exposes a limitation that a successful fit and interpretable coefficient do not remove.')

L(1, '1. MAE: average the absolute size of misses',
  'Compute mean absolute error and read it in the target unit.',
  [('mean_absolute_error; MAE', 'MAE averages absolute differences between paired actual and predicted values. It uses the target unit and smaller is better.'),
   ('Typical versus guaranteed error', 'A mean summarises the evaluated sample. It does not mean every case lies within that distance or provide a prediction interval.')],
  'Take each signed residual, remove the sign and average the magnitudes. MAE answers how many cups predictions missed by on average over these evaluated cases. It is easy to communicate because the unit remains cups.\n\nMAE does not distinguish underprediction from overprediction, and it does not guarantee that every event is close. A model can have many small errors and a few very large errors; inspect the distribution as well as the average. Report the number of evaluated cases and use the same cases for every model comparison.\n\nThe example computes MAE by hand and with scikit-learn. This is a metric calculation, not a fitted model. Keep actual and predicted values in the same order. A mathematically correct metric applied to mismatched rows still gives a meaningless result. The final project uses MAE as its predeclared model-selection criterion and reports other metrics for context.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nactual = np.array([100.0, 120.0, 90.0])\npredicted = np.array([95.0, 130.0, 88.0])\nabsolute = np.abs(actual - predicted)\nprint("Absolute errors:", absolute.tolist())\nprint("Manual MAE:", round(float(absolute.mean()), 3))\nprint("Library MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error(actual, predicted), 3), "cups")',
  'Calculate MAE for actual[10,20,30] and predicted[12,18,33].',
  'from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nprint("MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error([10, 20, 30], [12, 18, 33]), 3))',
  'Show two models with equal MAE but different error concentration.',
  'import numpy as np\nfor residual in [[3.0, 3.0, 3.0], [0.0, 0.0, 9.0]]:\n    errors = np.abs(residual)\n    print("Errors:", residual, "MAE:", float(errors.mean()), "largest:", float(errors.max()))',
  [('If MAE is8cups, is every error at most8?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for linear regression', 'An average is not a maximum or an uncertainty interval.'),
   ('What unit does MAE use for cup demand?', 'Cups', 'Cups squared', 'A percentage automatically', 'Absolute differences retain the original target unit.')],
  'MAE8 means every future prediction is within8cups.', 'State average held-out error, sample size and the observed error distribution.',
  'The absolute misses are5,10 and2cups; their sum17 divided by3 gives the printed MAE. Both calculations use corresponding rows in the same order.')

L(1, '2. MSE and RMSE: give large errors more weight',
  'Compute squared-error metrics and explain their units.',
  [('mean_squared_error; MSE', 'MSE averages squared residuals. If the target is cups, MSE is in cups squared.'),
   ('root_mean_squared_error; RMSE', 'RMSE is the square root of MSE and returns to the target unit. It emphasises larger misses more than MAE does.')],
  'MSE is the per-row average of the least-squares error cost. A twenty-cup miss contributes four times the squared cost of a ten-cup miss. This sensitivity can be desirable when large errors are particularly harmful, but it can also make the score strongly affected by unusual records.\n\nRMSE takes the square root so the final unit is cups again. It is not the average of per-row square roots of squared error; that latter calculation is MAE. The order of operations matters. For the same nonnegative error magnitudes, RMSE is at least MAE, with equality when all magnitudes are equal.\n\nDo not compare raw MSE and MAE numbers as though they share a unit. A model with MSE100cups² and MAE8cups does not have a larger average error simply because100 is larger than8. Use the metric definition, the target scale and the task’s cost to interpret the result.',
  'import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, root_mean_squared_error\ny, p = [100.0, 100.0, 100.0], [98.0, 98.0, 86.0]\nprint("MAE:", round(mean_absolute_error(y, p), 3), "cups")\nprint("MSE:", round(mean_squared_error(y, p), 3), "cups squared")\nprint("RMSE:", round(root_mean_squared_error(y, p), 3), "cups")\nprint("Root of MSE:", round(float(np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y, p))), 3))',
  'Compute MSE and RMSE for residual magnitudes3 and4.',
  'import numpy as np\ne = np.array([3.0, 4.0])\nmse = float((e ** 2).mean())\nprint("MSE:", mse)\nprint("RMSE:", round(float(np.sqrt(mse)), 3))',
  'Compare the squared contributions of ten-cup and twenty-cup misses.',
  'print("Ten-cup squared cost:", 10 ** 2)\nprint("Twenty-cup squared cost:", 20 ** 2)\nprint("Ratio:", 20 ** 2 / 10 ** 2)',
  [('What is the MSE unit if target demand uses cups?', 'Cups squared', 'Cups', 'Percent correct', 'Squaring residuals squares their unit.'),
   ('Is RMSE the mean of absolute errors?', 'No; it is the root of mean squared errors', 'Yes', 'Only when all errors differ', 'Root-after-averaging squared errors differs from absolute-error averaging.')],
  'Take the mean of square roots of squared residuals and call it RMSE.', 'Average squared residuals first, then take one square root.',
  'The example has two small misses and one much larger miss. RMSE gives that large miss more influence than MAE while retaining the cup unit.')

L(1, '3. R-squared is not percentage accuracy',
  'Interpret positive, zero and negative R-squared and handle the constant-target edge case.',
  [('r2_score; coefficient of determination', 'For nonconstant evaluated targets, R-squared =1−SSE/SST, where SST measures squared deviation from the evaluated target mean. It can be negative.'),
   ('Constant-target edge case; force_finite', 'R-squared is mathematically undefined when evaluated targets have no variation. scikit-learn’s default finite replacement gives1 for perfect constant predictions and0 otherwise; report the context.')],
  'R-squared compares squared prediction error with squared deviations from the evaluated sample’s own mean. One is perfect on these rows. Zero matches that evaluated-mean reference in squared error. A negative result means the predictions are worse than that reference; it does not mean a negative percentage of correct labels.\n\nThe evaluated-mean reference in the formula is not necessarily the same as a deployable DummyRegressor fitted on the training mean. You may report both, but name them correctly. R-squared also depends on how much target variation exists in the evaluated sample, so values from unrelated datasets are not directly comparable as universal model quality.\n\nIf every actual target is equal, the formula’s denominator is zero. The library’s default force_finite behaviour avoids non-finite values in routine evaluation, but that convention must not conceal the degenerate sample. The challenge detects the zero denominator explicitly without generating warning messages. A regression estimator’s score method commonly reports R-squared, unlike the classifier accuracy from Week44.',
  'from sklearn.metrics import r2_score\ny = [10.0, 20.0, 30.0]\nfor name, p in {"perfect": y, "evaluated mean": [20.0] * 3, "poor": [50.0] * 3}.items():\n    print(name, round(r2_score(y, p), 3))',
  'Reconstruct R-squared manually for actual[1,2,3] and predicted[1,2,4].',
  'import numpy as np\ny, p = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]), np.array([1.0, 2.0, 4.0])\nsse = float(((y - p) ** 2).sum())\nsst = float(((y - y.mean()) ** 2).sum())\nprint("R-squared:", 1 - sse / sst)',
  'Detect that R-squared needs special interpretation for a constant target.',
  'import numpy as np\ny = np.array([5.0, 5.0, 5.0])\nsst = float(((y - y.mean()) ** 2).sum())\nprint("SST:", sst)\nprint("Undefined formula denominator:", sst == 0)',
  [('Can R-squared be negative?', 'Yes; worse than the evaluated-mean squared-error reference', 'Never', 'Only when targets are negative', 'A prediction can have SSE larger than SST.'),
   ('Is R-squared0.8 the same as80% of regression predictions being correct?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for cup counts', 'R-squared measures relative squared error, not a classification correct-count percentage.')],
  'Describe R-squared as percentage accuracy or assume it always lies from0to1.', 'State its squared-error reference, possible negative values and target-variation context.',
  'The same actual labels are used for all three vectors. Constant20 is the evaluated mean and gives zero; the poor constant50 has a much larger squared error and therefore a negative result.')

L(1, '4. Choose and fit a regression baseline',
  'Use training-only mean or median references and explain their metric relationship.',
  [('DummyRegressor; mean versus median', 'A constant mean prediction minimises training squared error among constants. A constant median minimises training absolute error among constants.'),
   ('make_data; make_splits', 'The supplied lab generates independent fictional events locally. make_splits reserves72 final rows and returns288 training rows; examples develop only within that training portion unless explicitly reporting the final test.')],
  'Regression needs a baseline just as classification does. DummyRegressor can predict the training mean or training median for every new case. Fitting the constant on evaluation labels would unfairly use the answers. Choose the reference according to the task and primary metric, then compare on the same held-out rows.\n\nThe training mean is the least-squares constant; the training median is an absolute-error constant optimum. Neither guarantees the lowest loss on every new sample. The example contains one large training value, so mean and median differ substantially. This makes the metric relationship easier to inspect.\n\nThe final project predeclares a training-median baseline because model selection uses MAE. It still reports MSE, RMSE and R-squared for both baseline and model. Different metrics can rank alternatives differently; do not switch the winning metric after seeing which one makes your preferred model look best.',
  'from sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\nX = [[0], [1], [2], [3]]\ny = [10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 90.0]\nfor strategy in ["mean", "median"]:\n    m = DummyRegressor(strategy=strategy).fit(X, y)\n    p = m.predict(X)\n    print(strategy, "constant", float(p[0]), "training MAE", mean_absolute_error(y, p), "training MSE", mean_squared_error(y, p))',
  'Fit a median baseline on the lab training rows and print its learned constant without reading final test answers.',
  'from lab import make_splits\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\nd = make_splits()\nm = DummyRegressor(strategy="median").fit(d["X_train"], d["y_train"])\nprint("Training median cups:", float(m.constant_.ravel()[0]))',
  'Show that a fitted baseline predicts the same value for different new inputs.',
  'from sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\nm = DummyRegressor(strategy="median").fit([[1], [2], [3]], [10.0, 20.0, 90.0])\nprint(m.predict([[5], [100]]).tolist())',
  [('Which constant minimises training absolute error?', 'A training median', 'Always the largest value', 'The final test mean', 'The median is an absolute-loss optimum among constants.'),
   ('May a baseline use final test labels to fit its constant?', 'No', 'Yes because it is simple', 'Only for the median', 'Even trivial baselines need the training/evaluation boundary.')],
  'Fit the baseline constant from final test answers and compare it as a deployable reference.', 'Fit the reference on training labels and evaluate identical held-out rows.',
  'The outlying90 pulls the training mean to30 while the median stays10. Their different training MAE/MSE values demonstrate why metric choice matters.')

L(1, '5. Inspect error distribution and signed bias',
  'Report counts, typical misses, large misses and directional error together.',
  [('Error quantile; percentile', 'A quantile summarises a position in the observed error distribution. A90th-percentile absolute error describes this sample, not a guaranteed future coverage level.'),
   ('Mean signed residual; bias diagnostic', 'An average actual-minus-predicted residual reveals directional over/underprediction in these evaluated cases. It is not the formal statistical bias term by itself.')],
  'Two models can have the same MAE and very different planning risks. One can miss by a little on every event; another can be accurate most days and fail badly on one large event. Report an error distribution and inspect the important cases, keeping the evaluation set fixed.\n\nSigned mean residual gives direction. Positive means underprediction on average under our convention. Absolute quantiles describe magnitude, and maximum error identifies the largest observed miss. No one summary replaces the others. A90th percentile from ten rows is especially unstable and is not a calibrated prediction interval.\n\nDo not delete the largest error because it makes the score inconvenient. Investigate whether it is a genuine difficult case, a unit mismatch or a verified data error. Any correction needs a documented rule and appropriate evaluation treatment. In the final project, the CSV preserves every test row so reported metrics can be independently recalculated.',
  'import numpy as np\nr = np.array([-2.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 20.0])\na = np.abs(r)\nprint("Rows:", len(r))\nprint("Mean signed:", float(r.mean()))\nprint("MAE:", float(a.mean()))\nprint("90th percentile absolute:", round(float(np.quantile(a, 0.9)), 3))\nprint("Largest observed miss:", float(a.max()))',
  'Find the event id with the largest absolute error without removing it.',
  'errors = {"event A": -2.0, "event B": 15.0, "event C": 3.0}\nworst = max(errors, key=lambda name: abs(errors[name]))\nprint("Investigate:", worst, errors[worst], "cups")',
  'Show that zero signed average can coexist with nonzero MAE.',
  'import numpy as np\nr = np.array([-8.0, 8.0])\nprint("Signed average:", float(r.mean()))\nprint("MAE:", float(np.abs(r).mean()))',
  [('Does an observed error percentile guarantee the same future coverage?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only on five rows', 'A sample quantile is descriptive, not automatically a calibrated interval.'),
   ('Should the largest genuine error be removed to improve the headline score?', 'No; investigate and report it', 'Yes', 'Only if it hurts RMSE', 'Deleting difficult cases changes the evaluation question.')],
  'Publish one average and hide the largest misses or their direction.', 'Report the denominator, signed pattern, magnitudes and traceable difficult cases.',
  'The20-cup underprediction changes both average magnitude and direction. The quantile and maximum expose concentration of error that a single mean would hide.')

L(1, '6. Match the metric to the planning decision',
  'Choose a primary criterion before comparison and distinguish asymmetric costs.',
  [('Asymmetric error cost', 'Underprediction and overprediction may have different consequences. A declared cost can weight positive and negative residuals differently.'),
   ('Predeclared primary metric', 'The criterion used to select a model is chosen before inspecting final results. Additional metrics provide context rather than an opportunity to select a preferred story.')],
  'Suppose being short one cup has a different practical cost from making one extra cup. MAE treats both directions symmetrically, while an explicitly asymmetric planning loss can reflect this distinction. Choose and document the decision context before selecting a model.\n\nThis week selects the final model by mean cross-validation MAE so the comparison is simple and reproducible. It also reports MSE, RMSE and R-squared. The example below is a separate cost illustration using arbitrary teaching weights, not a recommendation for a real café’s costs. A low MAE alone does not establish a stock policy or capacity plan.\n\nAn estimator’s fitting objective can differ from the selection metric. LinearRegression fits squared training loss, while the project compares models using held-out absolute error. That is a legitimate declared workflow; it does not mean the estimator directly minimises the business cost. More specialised objectives and uncertainty-aware decisions need their own evidence.',
  'import numpy as np\nactual = np.array([100.0, 100.0])\nfor name, prediction in {"low": [90.0, 90.0], "high": [110.0, 110.0]}.items():\n    r = actual - np.array(prediction)\n    cost = 3 * np.maximum(r, 0) + np.maximum(-r, 0)\n    print(name, "MAE", float(np.abs(r).mean()), "teaching cost", float(cost.mean()))',
  'Record MAE as primary and RMSE/R-squared as secondary before comparison.',
  'plan = {"select_by": "mean training-only CV MAE", "also_report": ["MSE", "RMSE", "R-squared"], "test": "reserved until selection"}\nprint(plan)',
  'Compute a two-per-unit shortage cost and one-per-unit surplus cost for residuals[5,-3].',
  'import numpy as np\nr = np.array([5.0, -3.0])\ncost = 2 * np.maximum(r, 0) + np.maximum(-r, 0)\nprint("Per-case teaching cost:", cost.tolist())\nprint("Mean cost:", float(cost.mean()))',
  [('When should the primary model-selection metric be chosen?', 'Before final result inspection', 'After finding a metric that favours the preferred model', 'Only after deployment', 'Predeclaring the criterion avoids cherry-picking the reported winner.'),
   ('Does ordinary MAE distinguish equal under- and overprediction magnitudes?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when target values are positive', 'Absolute error is symmetric in the residual sign.')],
  'Try every metric and announce whichever makes the preferred model win.', 'Select with the declared criterion and report complementary evidence honestly.',
  'Both prediction vectors miss by10cups, so MAE ties. The arbitrary shortage weighting makes the low prediction more costly, illustrating why prediction quality and a planning decision are not identical.')
